  "description": "One-shot: produce a working, theme-bound login page on the current app.\n\nWhy use this over hand-composing the form: composing a login card primitive-by-primitive (Grid \u2192 Grid \u2192 Text \u2192 TextBox \u2192 TextBox \u2192 Button) is a common doom-loop \u2014 agents miss the `bindingPath` shape, forget `updateStoreImmediately`, get the `UIEngine.Login` arg name wrong (`email` vs the required `userName`), or accidentally add a Navigate step after success (which fights the platform's automatic login-as-wrapper substitution). This tool composes the entire page + the `handleLogin` event function from a known-good template in one call. Idempotent \u2014 call it again to reset a broken login page.\n\nThe result:\n- A page named `login` (or whatever you pass as `name`) with a centered card on the page background.\n- Email + password inputs bound to `Page.email` / `Page.password` with `updateStoreImmediately: true`.\n- A Sign In button wired to `handleLogin`, which calls `UIEngine.Login(userName, password, identifierType=\"EMAIL_ID\", rememberMe=true)` and writes `Page.loginError` on error. No navigate on success \u2014 the platform re-renders the originally-requested URL natively once auth succeeds.\n- An error Text whose visibility is bound to `Page.loginError`.\n\nAfter calling this, you MUST also:\n1. `update_app(app_code=..., properties={\"loginPage\": \"login\", \"defaultPage\": \"<home page name>\"})` so the platform knows to substitute this page when an anonymous user requests a protected page.\n2. Set `permission: \"Authorities.Logged_IN\"` on any page that requires authentication (via `update_page`).\n\nCustomer-facing apps additionally need `configure_app_for_customer_signup` so end-users can register without a builder grant.",
